1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,899
Infection Chains in LLM Swarms: Spread, Detection and Layered Gating.

2
00:00:06,149 --> 00:00:10,824
Apart Research Swarm Alignment Hackathon. Agustin Diaz-Cano.

3
00:00:11,074 --> 00:00:14,688
One compromised agent can talk its peers into helping it.

4
00:00:14,938 --> 00:00:20,596
Per-agent monitors see one message at a time, so each request can look like routine admin work.

5
00:00:20,846 --> 00:00:24,955
We measure how the infection spreads, and what actually stops it.

6
00:00:25,205 --> 00:00:32,295
Ten agents, ten rounds. A 12-character canary credential is split into three 4-character fragments.

7
00:00:32,545 --> 00:00:38,176
The fragments sit at least two hops from patient zero, so no single message holds the secret.

8
00:00:38,426 --> 00:00:43,086
Patient zero must recruit intermediaries, and every hop looks harmless.

9
00:00:43,336 --> 00:00:49,335
Ring and complete networks. Three persuasion styles; the third is held out from training.

10
00:00:49,585 --> 00:00:55,448
96 real episodes: 3 pilot, 15 training, 78 test.

11
00:00:55,698 --> 00:01:02,132
Gemma 4 31B plays the agents. DeepSeek V4 Flash is the guardrail.

12
00:01:02,382 --> 00:01:08,302
7,944 model calls logged in the test analysis, under one credit.

13
00:01:08,552 --> 00:01:16,495
Preregistered, separate train and test seeds, deterministic grading, 100-message audit at 100% agreement.

14
00:01:16,745 --> 00:01:24,950
The infection spreads: by round nine, 6 to 6.5 of 10 agents are infected on a ring, 4.5 on the complete graph.

15
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:29,893
Splitting the secret does not stop the infection. It makes theft harder.

16
00:01:30,143 --> 00:01:36,623
Split credential: full exfiltration in 1 of 6 ring episodes, 0 of 6 on the complete graph.

17
00:01:36,873 --> 00:01:40,912
Solo credential: 3 of 6 and 2 of 6.

18
00:01:41,162 --> 00:01:45,451
30 clean episodes, with and without gates: nothing leaked.

19
00:01:45,701 --> 00:01:51,200
D1 is a lexical classifier. D2 is a micro-LLM rubric guardrail.

20
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:56,128
D3 adds graph context: suspicion travels along message edges.

21
00:01:56,378 --> 00:02:01,012
D5 is a tiered filter that calls the LLM only for ambiguous messages.

22
00:02:01,262 --> 00:02:05,771
Each gate is calibrated on training data to a 0.5% false positive rate.

23
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:10,784
Offline AUROC is near 0.998 on seen playbooks.

24
00:02:11,034 --> 00:02:20,766
But the realized false positive rate was 6.4%, not 0.5%, and held-out data had no negatives, so we claim nothing on distribution shift.

25
00:02:21,016 --> 00:02:29,674
Online, gated runs leaked in 0 of 6 episodes versus 1 of 6 unprotected. Not significant: McNemar p equals 1.

26
00:02:29,924 --> 00:02:39,059
Utility loss stayed under 0.02. No hypothesis is clearly confirmed, and H2 was not identifiable. We report that.

27
00:02:39,309 --> 00:02:46,127
Infection chains are measurable. At this sample size, a per-message guard alone is not evidence of safety.

28
00:02:46,377 --> 00:02:52,585
Playbooks stay out of the repository, the canary is synthetic, every number comes from committed logs.

29
00:02:52,835 --> 00:02:59,074
Next: more seeds, adaptive attackers, mixed-model swarms. Thank you.
